THE SAME REPORT, TWO LINE ENDINGS
   dos   69 64 2c 61 6d 6f 75 6e 74 0d 0a 31 ...   28 bytes
   unix  69 64 2c 61 6d 6f 75 6e 74 0a 31 2c ...   25 bytes

READ BOTH WITHOUT TELLING PYTHON ANYTHING
   dos   last value '3.25'     float() -> 3.25   key 'amount'
   unix  last value '3.25'     float() -> 3.25   key 'amount'

   The numbers are identical and correct to the cent. Now look at the
   values as bytes -- that is where the difference is hiding.

WHERE THE EXTRA BYTE GOES
   csv.reader with newline='' -> [['id', 'amount'], ['1', '10.50'], ['2', '3.25']]
   split('\n')                 -> [['id', 'amount\r'], ['1', '10.50\r'], ['2', '3.25\r'], ['']]
   Splitting by hand leaves a 0d on the end of every last field. It
   never shows on screen, it survives str.strip() only if you remember
   to call it, and it makes '10.50\r' a string that float() still
   accepts -- so the totals come out right and the KEYS do not match.

THE COMPARISON THAT FAILS
   dos id field '1' == '1' ? True
   That is the shape of the whole bug: arithmetic is fine because the
   trailing byte is stripped by the number parser, and lookups fail
   because they are not.

THE THREE FIXES, IN ORDER OF PREFERENCE
   1. open(p, newline='') and let the csv module handle it -- it knows
      about \r\n and it is the documented way to open a CSV.
   2. open(p) in text mode with universal newlines, which translates
      \r\n to \n before your code sees it -- fine for line-oriented
      data, wrong for csv, which needs to see the raw line endings.
   3. .rstrip('\r\n') by hand, which works and which you will forget
      exactly once.

AND THE ONE THAT IS NOT A FIX
   'a\r\nb'.splitlines() -> 'a\r\nb'.splitlines() = ['a', 'b']
   splitlines() cuts on eleven different sequences, not one, so it will
   also split a string containing U+2028 or a form feed. Right for text,
   wrong for a format whose record separator is defined.
